        {
          "ruleId": "cuda_init_failure_is_fatal_in_the_driver_sample",
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "The one device-behaviour claim in the head commit is that a failed CUDA driver initialisation means there is no device to measure on, so K1 must fail rather than skip. The NVIDIA corpus returns the driver-API init sample, where cuInit(0) is followed by cuDeviceGetCount and a count of 0 or a failed init ends the program with exit(EXIT_FAILURE): the reference code treats it as fatal, not as a reason to go on. This supports the direction of the change; it does not say what a test harness should do."
          },
          "properties": {
            "grounding": "cited",
            "source": "nvidia-cuda-docs MCP search_cuda_docs, query: cuInit returns CUDA_ERROR_NO_DEVICE when no CUDA-capable device is present; driver API initialization (cudaDeviceInit sample, cuda.h)",
            "excerpt": "CUresult err = cuInit(0); int deviceCount = 0; if (CUDA_SUCCESS == err) checkCudaErrors(cuDeviceGetCount(&deviceCount)); if (deviceCount == 0) { fprintf(stderr, \"cudaDeviceInit error: no devices supporting CUDA\\n\"); exit(EXIT_FAILURE); }",
            "excerpt_sha256": "a150549e4fc18a8277805fdce2108f090e39940daca462e05443782f319ca844",
            "failure_scenario": "If an init failure were an expected, recoverable state in the driver contract, a skip could be the right reading of it; the sample treats it as fatal.",
            "precision_class": "advisory"
          }
        }
      ]
